# =============================================================
# counterparties.yml.example — the framework-shippable TWIN
# =============================================================
# Copy to instance/config/counterparties.yml and describe YOUR counterparties.
# The entry below is a PLACEHOLDER: consent ships `pending` — a fresh instance
# must never arrive with a pre-consented party. Same law as peers.yml.example,
# applied to people and organisations instead of peer Cabinets.
#
# A COUNTERPARTY is a party the org has a standing relationship with who is
# NOT the Captain. The Captain is the authority root and is never listed here.
#
# Loaded by framework/channels/counterparty.py. Registry-as-DATA: it stamps
# WHO a send went to into the audit ledger. It renders no authority verdict
# and gates no send today — `outbound_permitted()` exists and is honest, but
# nothing consults it yet (on a cabinet with no registry it answers False for
# everyone, so wiring it into send() is a separate Captain-gated act).
#
# Schema per entry (the map key is the id):
#   <id>                  — slug ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$; this is the ONLY
#                           name stamped into the ledger, so keep it opaque
#                           enough for an audit record
#   kind                  — person | org
#   relationship          — customer | supplier | employee | contractor |
#                           partner | advisor | regulator | other
#   handles               — REQUIRED, non-empty list of addresses or opaque
#                           chat/member ids. Case-folded on load, so
#                           Ada@Acme.com and ada@acme.com are ONE party.
#                           A handle claimed by two entries makes the WHOLE
#                           file corrupt — a consent record may not guess.
#   consent               — granted | pending | withdrawn. Ships `pending`.
#                           Only the Captain moves an entry to `granted`.
#                           `withdrawn` always beats scope.
#                           (`unknown` is the ABSENCE answer for an
#                           unregistered handle and may never be written here.)
#   captain_scope         — REQUIRED mapping with exactly one key:
#       channels          — the adapter names this party may be contacted on
#                           (e.g. teams, outlook, slack). There is NO wildcard
#                           by design. An EMPTY list is legal and means
#                           "consented, but no channel authorized yet".
#   display_name          — OPTIONAL, free text, for humans. NEVER journaled.
#   notes                 — OPTIONAL, free text, for humans. NEVER journaled.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: an unknown key, a wrong version, an off-vocabulary value, a
# duplicate handle, or unparseable YAML loads as the EMPTY registry — every
# recipient is then an unresolved party with `unknown` consent. One typo does
# not best-effort-consent the rest of the file.
#
# The live instance/config/counterparties.yml is deployment-created and
# untracked; only this placeholder twin is tracked, so no real person ever
# enters the repo.

version: 1

counterparties:
  example-counterparty:
    display_name: Example Counterparty
    kind: org
    relationship: partner
    handles:
      - contact@example.invalid
    consent: pending          # Captain flips to `granted` to enable contact
    captain_scope:
      channels: []            # add adapter names once the Captain scopes them
    notes: >
      Placeholder party — replace with your real counterparty and keep
      consent `pending` until the Captain rules the relationship live.
